<pdd-reason>The application root. It fetches the live board, keeps it moving as votes and stage changes arrive, and holds the frozen layout every other component slots into.</pdd-reason>

<pdd-interface>
{"type":"module","module":{"functions":[
  {"name":"AppShell","signature":"function AppShell(): JSX.Element","returns":"the application root: chyron, masthead, tabs, the four tab bodies, sandbox panel, dialogs and toast"}
]}}
</pdd-interface>

<pdd-dependency>prompts/frontend/api_client_typescript.prompt</pdd-dependency>
<pdd-dependency>prompts/frontend/components/broadcast_typescriptreact.prompt</pdd-dependency>
<pdd-dependency>prompts/frontend/components/feature_card_typescriptreact.prompt</pdd-dependency>
<pdd-dependency>prompts/frontend/components/sandbox_panel_typescriptreact.prompt</pdd-dependency>
<pdd-dependency>prompts/frontend/components/submit_modal_typescriptreact.prompt</pdd-dependency>
<pdd-dependency>prompts/frontend/components/my_pitches_modal_typescriptreact.prompt</pdd-dependency>

Implement `frontend/src/app_shell.tsx`. It imports the frozen `./styles.css` once for the whole
application.

<responsibility>
Fetch the board from the live backend, render it in the frozen layout, and keep it current as the pipeline moves.
</responsibility>

<non_responsibilities>
This module DOES NOT call `fetch` or Supabase directly; every request and subscription goes through `api_client`.
This module DOES NOT render a card's internals, the chyron's cycling, the preview embed, or a dialog's fields — those components own themselves.
This module DOES NOT ship a mock or fixture mode; the board is read live from the backend.
This module DOES NOT validate a pitch; `SubmitModal` owns field rules and the backend owns the verdict.
This module DOES NOT resolve or validate the preview URL; `api_client` owns configuration and the host allowlist.
</non_responsibilities>

<vocabulary>
Recent: of the ticker tail, the newest handful of broadcast rows — enough to fill the strip on load, not a history.
**View** — one of the four tabs: `pipeline`, `shipped`, `holding`, `vault`. Each is a different question about the same board, answered by the same endpoint.
**Live** — the board follows the pipeline without a reload, because the daemon's inserts and the vote counter's updates both arrive over Realtime.
**Pitch Coin** — one unit of the daily submission allowance, held as a client-side counter. The backend's `429` is the only real limit.
Active: of a tab, the one view currently being fetched and rendered. It carries `is-active` and `aria-current`; exactly one tab is active at any moment.
Successful: of a pitch, an `onPitch` that resolved `ok`, meaning the backend returned `202` and the pitch is queued in Redis. It is not yet screened, and it is not yet on the board.
</vocabulary>

<capabilities>
- MAY hold board state, the active view/sort/filters, vote-in-flight state, the voted-id set, the coin balance, dialog open state, and the toast.
- MAY call `api_client` functions and subscribe through it.
- MUST NOT call `fetch`, construct a Supabase client, or read `import.meta.env`.
- MUST NOT add a hex value, font, radius, shadow, or animation — compose only classes from the design guide's closed set.
</capabilities>

<contract_rules>
R1 (MUST): Wrap everything in a single `div.app` and keep the frozen skeleton in order — `Broadcast`, then `header.masthead`, then `div.layout` holding the feed column and the `SandboxPanel`. The responsive collapses at 960px and 560px are already in the stylesheet and MUST NOT be reimplemented inline.
R2 (MUST): Render the masthead brand as `upvote·dev` with the middle dot in `brand-dot`, and the tagline `You wish it. Agents ship it.` These are the product's name and promise, not placeholder copy for the compiler to replace.
R3 (MUST): Render `masthead-actions` as the coin chip, then `🔒 My pitches` with a `pitch-count` badge when unresolved pitches exist, then `🔥 Pitch a feature`. The two controls are a matched pair and carry **both** of their classes — `btn-ghost btn-mypitches` and `btn-primary btn-pitch`. `btn-mypitches` on its own sets only flex alignment, so omitting `btn-ghost` renders the control as bare text with no border, padding, or radius beside a fully-styled pill.
R4 (MUST): Render the four tabs — `⚡ Up Next Pipeline`, `🏆 Shipped`, `⏸ Holding Pattern`, `🗄️ The Vault` — mapping to `pipeline`, `shipped`, `holding`, `vault`, with the active one carrying `is-active` and `aria-current`, and a `tab-count` on Holding when it is non-empty. A held feature is the one thing on this board that looks like a loss, so the count is what stops it being quietly buried.
R5 (MUST): On the pipeline tab render the `feed-controls` sort toggle (`🔥 Highest Upvotes` → `top`, `✨ Newest` → `new`). No sort other than `top` and `new` exists server-side and inventing one earns a `400`.
R5a (MUST): Render `filter-chips` as a **sibling of `feed-controls`, on its own row** — `All`, then one chip per pipeline stage — and MUST NOT nest it inside `feed-controls`. `feed-controls` is a flex row, so nesting crushes the sort toggle and six stage chips onto one line, which is the cramped layout this rule exists to prevent.
R5b (MUST): Let the chips **multi-select**: each toggles independently, `All` clears the set and is active only while the set is empty, and an empty set means no filter. Stage filtering asks "show me these kinds", and one-at-a-time answers a narrower question than anyone asked.
R6 (MUST): Send the whole selected set as the `status` query parameter rather than filtering the fetched page in the browser. Client-side filtering of one page silently hides matching rows that sit on the next page, which reads as the filter being broken.
R7 (MUST): On the vault tab render the `vault-search` input and send its value as `q`, debounced, so the endpoint does the narrowing. A search box that only filters what is already loaded is worse than no search box.
R8 (MUST): Fetch through `api_client.listFeatures` on mount and whenever the view, sort, filters, or query change, and render the card matching the view — `FeatureCard` on pipeline, `ShippedCard` on shipped, `HoldingCard` on holding, `VaultCard` on vault. The board is read live; there is no fixture path.
R9 (MUST): Render three distinct states — loading, empty, and error — using `feed-status`. An error MUST NOT render as an empty board: "no features in this stage right now" and "we could not reach the server" are opposite messages, and showing the first when the second is true makes a broken deploy look like a quiet community.
R10 (MUST): Show the backend's `message` verbatim on an error, with a retry control. The copy is written to be displayed.
R11 (MUST): Render the section blurb for the shipped, holding, and vault tabs from the design guide's copy deck. Those three tabs each need a sentence of framing — especially holding, where the blurb is what stops a paused idea reading as a rejected one.
R12 (MUST): Subscribe once through `api_client.subscribe` and dispose on unmount using the returned function. Every component subscribing for itself multiplies channels and leaks them.
R13 (MUST): On `onFeatureUpdate`, merge the incoming row into the matching feature in state rather than replacing it. Realtime sends the changed Postgres row, not the joined shape the endpoint builds, so `children` is absent on an update — replacing wholesale would blank a split parent's children (api_client R19).
R14 (MUST): On `onFeatureInsert`, branch on `parent_id`: a null parent is a new top-level card; a set parent means a split child, which is merged into that parent's `children` rather than added to the feed. The read endpoint returns root rows only, and a child appearing as a sibling of its parent contradicts the board it came from.
R15 (MUST): Ignore a Realtime row that does not belong to the current view — an `ARCHIVED` update while the pipeline tab is open leaves the pipeline alone. The subscription is board-wide but each tab asks a narrower question.
R16 (MUST): Send a vote through `api_client.upvote`, track the in-flight id so the card can disable its control, and reconcile the count from the response. On `409 already_voted`, mark the feature voted rather than showing an error — the visitor's intent is already recorded, so an error would be a lie.
R17 (MUST): Keep a set of ids this visitor has voted for, seeded from `viewer_has_voted` on every read, and pass it to every card, so a returning visitor is not invited to click something that will answer `409`.
R18 (MUST): Celebrate a child crossing its `unlock_threshold` with the `🔓 Unlocked: "{title}"` toast. The unlock is the reward the split was designed to produce and it passes silently without this.
R19 (MUST): Hold the coin balance as a **client-side counter** — seed it at five, decrement on a successful pitch, and refill two minutes after it empties, announcing the refill with a toast. No endpoint reports a remaining count; this is a demo economy tuned so a judge watching for five minutes sees the refill happen.
R20 (MUST): Disable the pitch control at zero coins and replace its label with `🪙 Coins refresh in {m:ss}`, ticking every second. A disabled button with no explanation reads as a broken app.
R21 (MUST): On a `429` set the local balance to zero and pass `resets_at` to the dialog. The local number was optimistic; the server's answer replaces it rather than arguing with it.
R22 (MUST): Own both dialogs' open state, pass `api_client.createFeature` as the pitch seam, and load `api_client.getMyPitches` when the My Pitches dialog opens.
R23 (MUST): On a successful pitch show the screening toast and add a **pending entry** built from the returned `feature_id` and the title the dialog passes back, so the wait is legible in the My Pitches dialog.
R24 (MUST NOT): Insert the new pitch into the feed, or render any pending entry inline with the board. It is unscreened until the daemon says otherwise, and putting it on the public board is the exact failure screening exists to prevent.
R25 (MUST): Remove a pending entry when a Realtime insert arrives whose `feature_id` matches it, and celebrate the arrival. That moment — pitch, wait, appear — is the loop the product is selling.
R25a (MUST): Do this **first in the insert handler, before any guard that can return early** — before the `parent_id` branch, before the current-view check, and before the filter-chip check. Whether an author's own pitch is still "screening" is a fact about their pitch, not about which tab they are looking at. Gating it behind those guards leaves the pitch listed as screening *and* present as a live feature in the same dialog, which is what shipped: the author sees their idea twice and only a page reload fixes it.
R26 (MUST): Raise the sandbox pulse when the chyron reports its success phase, and clear it when the panel reports a refresh. That is the chain that turns "an agent shipped something" into a visitor actually looking at it.
R27 (MUST): Render at most one toast at a time in a `toast` with `role="status"`, auto-dismissing after a few seconds, and clear any pending timer on replacement so a fast sequence cannot dismiss the newest message early.
R28 (MUST NOT): Block first render on the anonymous session. Board reads are anonymous, so the feed must paint before any auth work completes — the session is only needed when someone votes.
R29 (MUST): Give the feed a landmark role and an accessible name, and announce loading and error states to assistive technology rather than only styling them.
R30 (MUST NOT): Introduce a CSS framework, UI kit, or state-management library. Component state and context are sufficient at this size, and the design guide forbids the first two outright.
R32 (MUST): Feed the chyron the live pipeline: load the recent tail with `api_client.listBroadcastEvents` on mount, append every `onBroadcastEvent` row from the same subscription opened in R12, and pass the result to `Broadcast` as `messages`. Until this is wired the strip cycles its scripted fallback, and the product's central claim — that you can watch the agents work — is an animation.
R33 (MUST): Map a broadcast row to the ticker's shape by agent name, choosing the icon from the agent (`Guardagent` 🛡️, `PM Agent` 🔮, `Architect Agent` 📐, `Janitor Agent` 🧹, `Ship Agent` 🚀) and marking a row `success` when its phase is `deployed`. The relay already translated internal phases into public ones; this is presentation, not a second translation.
R34 (MUST): Leave `messages` unset while the list is empty, so `Broadcast` falls back to its own script rather than rendering an empty strip. A quiet pipeline is normal and the chyron is part of the frozen layout.
R35 (MUST): Cap the retained list at a few dozen rows, dropping the oldest. The ticker is a window on what is happening now, and an unbounded array grows for as long as the tab is open.
R31 (MUST): Handle a Vault reboot through `api_client.rebootFeature`, removing the row from the Vault on success and confirming with the `⚡ Rebooted! A fresh 30-day VOTING window has started.` toast. The row leaving the tab the visitor is looking at is not by itself legible as success, so the toast is the confirmation. It **persists** now (US-16): a `422 not_archived` means another visitor revived it first, which is a race worth reporting calmly rather than an error, and any other failure MUST leave the row in the Vault rather than pretending it moved.
</contract_rules>

<pdd>
**The vault reboot now persists (US-16).** `POST /api/features/{id}/reboot` moves the row back to
`VOTING`, resets `created_at`, restarts the count at 1 with the reviver's vote, and clears the old
votes. The shell's job is to remove the row from the Vault and confirm; the server owns the rest.
A `422 not_archived` is the two-visitors race, not a failure.

**Pagination is not wired.** `listFeatures` returns `next_cursor` and this shell ignores it, so each
tab shows the first page. The cursor is already threaded through the client, so it is a UI change
only.

**Realtime carries no auth context.** Postgres row events do not know who is watching, so
`viewer_has_voted` never arrives over the wire — the visitor's own vote state lives in the set from
R17 and is seeded by the REST read. A vote cast in another tab will not light up here until a
refetch.

**The chyron is live (US-11).** `listBroadcastEvents` loads the recent tail and `onBroadcastEvent`
appends rows as `backend/event_relay.py` writes them, so the strip shows the real pipeline.
`Broadcast`'s own default array remains only as the fallback for a quiet pipeline, which is why
`messages` is left unset when the list is empty (R34).
</pdd>

